#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199335} :activity/id #uuid "d96c3f54-0a5f-5ebf-a90e-04722ee6bf25" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049962 :topic/id #uuid "220386bf-4147-5f25-bbd8-b3b4f855f1a0" :topic/math-academy-id 4169} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199335 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199487 :tutorial/content "We've spent some time studying so-called **plane transformations** of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T: \\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^2.\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we'll generalize our understanding of derivatives to transformations (or functions) of the form \n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m.\n$$\n\nTo motivate the discussion, we start by considering functions of one variable. \n\nRecall that a function $f: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is **differentiable** at a point $x = a$ if there exists a number $f'(a)$, called the **derivative** of $f$ at $a$, such that\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f(x) - f(a)}{x - a}.\n$$\n\nWe can restate this definition by applying basic algebraic properties of limits.\n\nSince the left-hand side, $f'(a)$, is just a constant, we can rewrite the equation as\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to a} f'(a) = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f(x) - f(a)}{x - a},\n$$\n\nusing the fact that the limit of a constant is the constant itself.\n\nBringing all terms to one side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to a} \\left( \\frac{f(x) - f(a)}{x - a} - f'(a) \\right) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich can be rearranged as\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to a} \\left( \\frac{f(x) - f(a) - f'(a)(x - a)}{x - a} \\right) = 0.\n$$\n\nThis final expression tells us that the difference between $f(x)$ and its *linear approximation* $f(a) + f'(a)(x - a)$ becomes negligible relative to $x - a$ as $x$ approaches $a$.\n\nIn other words, not only does $f(x)$ become close to $f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)$ near $a$, but the error between them shrinks *faster than* the distance $|x - a|$ itself.\n\nLet's see how we can generalize this definition to vector-valued functions." :tutorial/id #uuid "a9769637-9fcb-5829-817e-f82930d6d99b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7762 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "087cad91-5bec-5130-91c0-60421a04d402" :step/math-academy-id 44919 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199362}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199336 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198411 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198053 :question/id #uuid "ea202ce3-07d4-5324-9ee4-f5617b430772" :question/math-academy-id "e-12477"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d912b296-6fd0-57c2-84e8-ca0b56549756" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049809}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427518}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Scalar Function"} :step/id #uuid "222e5898-b5c5-5d99-94e8-a487c966ad9c" :step/math-academy-id 44920 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199337}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199337 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198412 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198054 :question/id #uuid "ea76d21f-eff8-5634-975f-2b5bd24caf54" :question/math-academy-id "e-12478"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a818e04-786a-56b6-90cf-dcb573ccfa64" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048219}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401412}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Vector Function"} :step/id #uuid "9cc059b7-be28-5e30-9b41-3198b56bec94" :step/math-academy-id 44921 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199340}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199338 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198413 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198055 :question/id #uuid "db0be438-94fe-52de-9daf-60d1727ce7b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12479"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e90349c-33ae-5b60-9d0d-7542dfebbbc7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050293}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367790}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Plane to the Graph of a Scalar Function in Matrix Form"} :step/id #uuid "cc985165-12b8-5db0-9db3-3f6ef902bc46" :step/math-academy-id 44926 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199341}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199339 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198414 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198056 :question/id #uuid "f3540571-9159-50b4-a46c-4cf28a454602" :question/math-academy-id "e-12484"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f09fc28-bdce-554f-98f5-019d34542a13" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049012}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281597} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281617} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367832}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Affine Approximation of a Vector Function"} :step/id #uuid "2a4a2d6d-09ba-598c-aec7-fedd75d2da7e" :step/math-academy-id 44947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199340 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199488 :tutorial/content "The equation of the tangent plane to a surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0),$ where $z_0=f(x_0,y_0),$ is given by\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0).\n$$\n\nLet's express this equation in terms of the total derivative.\n\nWriting our multivariable function $f:\\mathbb R^2 \\to \\mathbb R$ as $f\\left(\\mathbf x \\right),$ where ![](images/88/88f9f758bffba384670449973c5c89edae05c67268d2914a12801b21b60a319f.svg) the total derivative of $f$ is given by\n\n![](images/56/56dc34676135e9c35b9e08cff732715c5d878d4a19f4fee6625384460ed6ee5f.svg)\n\nLet ![](images/ca/ca5b0074c216a3235f015d7b96dece9ffc6fc50eecbdd0b5e97dfbd89eb446fa.svg) Then, we can re-write the equation of the tangent plane as a matrix equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-{z}_{0} & =(x-{x}_{0})\\cdot \\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\cdot \\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) \\\\ z-{z}_{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x-{x}_{0} \\\\ y-{y}_{0}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ z-f({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\end{aligned}]\\cdot ([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{0} \\\\ {y}_{0}\\end{aligned}]) \\\\ z-f(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(a) & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(a)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (x-a) \\\\ z-f(a) & ={f}^{′}(a)(x-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane to $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf{a},$ in matrix form, is given by\n\n$$\nz - f(\\mathbf a) = \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "15747496-662a-5601-8abc-953fd6f5a43b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7764 :tutorial/title "The Tangent Plane to the Graph of a Scalar Function"} :step/id #uuid "67589fc2-05c5-57f9-9944-a77ae10109f1" :step/math-academy-id 44948 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199338}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199341 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199489 :tutorial/content "If $f:\\mathbb R \\to \\mathbb R$ is differentiable at $x=a,$ then for points close to $x=a,$ we have the linear approximation\n\n$$\nf(x) \\approx f(a) + f'(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nWe have a similar result for transformations of the form $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m.$\n\nIf $\\boldsymbol f:\\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R^m$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf x=\\mathbf a,$ then for points close to $\\mathbf x=\\mathbf a,$ we have the linear approximation\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf x) \\approx \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf a) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that this approximation for $\\boldsymbol f$ is affine. Thus, if a function is differentiable at a point $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a,$ it is **locally affine** in a neighborhood of $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a.$ \n\nLet's see some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "4f9fb273-0149-5dca-a967-7bb027abab86" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7765 :tutorial/title "Affine Approximations of Vector Functions"} :step/id #uuid "322a9f6a-d0e4-5c2f-b7dd-5ac1d040f24a" :step/math-academy-id 44949 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199339}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199362 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199495 :tutorial/content "Suppose that a transformation (or function) $\\boldsymbol f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](images/cd/cd9132d6a0e934349ae58a0718e6320cba3dd8ae84de7df2e3fb98b391256b57.svg) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions of $n$ variables. \nWe say that $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is **differentiable** at a point $\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ if there exists an $m \\times n$ matrix $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})$, called the **total derivative** of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{a}$, such that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x} \\to \\mathbf{a}}\n\\frac{\n\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}) - \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{a}) - \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})\n}{\n||\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}||\n}\n= \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nIn other words, near $\\mathbf{a}$, the function $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is well-approximated by the linear map\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{a}) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}),\n$$\n\nand the error between $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x})$ and this linear approximation becomes negligible compared to the distance $\\|\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}\\|$ as $\\mathbf{x}$ approaches $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nIf $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol f$ is given by the matrix\n\n![](images/6b/6bd7bcfb4e48c1833ff57c614bb18f469ca0a17e8a6e29d730b9e940c2f58984.svg)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We sometimes refer to $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf x)$ simply as **the derivative** of $\\boldsymbol f(\\mathbf x).$\n\n- For scalar-valued functions of the form $f: \\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R,$ the total derivative is the *transpose* of the gradient vector:\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a) = \\left(\\nabla f \\right)^T(\\mathbf a)\n$$\n\n- For vector-valued functions $\\boldsymbol f: \\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^n$ of a single parameter $t,$ the total derivative at $t=a$ is the usual column vector containing the partial derivatives of the components of $f{:}$\n![](images/27/27651b3ca026cad63ee6cbbd88162d3ce85b9afc642986daf0ae76c6519bdc38.svg)\n\n- For functions of the form $\\boldsymbol f: \\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^2,$ the *determinant* of this matrix is the corresponding Jacobian: \n\n$$\n\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)\\right)  = \\dfrac{\\partial (f_1,f_2)}{\\partial (x_1,x_2)}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for functions of the form $\\boldsymbol f: \\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3,$ the *determinant* of this matrix is the corresponding Jacobian: \n\n$$\n\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)\\right)  = \\dfrac{\\partial (f_1,f_2,f_3)}{\\partial (x_1,x_2,x_3)}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fb0be5f3-0f3d-5c1f-81f5-97faa1864dc1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7793 :tutorial/title "Defining the Total Derivative"} :step/id #uuid "19886282-4967-5610-ab9e-7c0b0a12ce44" :step/math-academy-id 45103 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199336}}] :activity/title "The Derivative of a Multivariable Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198497}]}
